From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 23:07:41 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 36/36] Camera: a map held in the hands as Minecraft holds it

A map was a flat HUD image in the middle of the screen, the same wherever
the player looked, with the hand hidden. Minecraft holds a filled map in
both hands when the other hand is empty (ItemInHandRenderer's
renderTwoHandedMap): almost flat (85 degrees) and half a block lower while
the player looks ahead, rising and turning to face the eyes as they look
down, upright from 49.5 degrees down. With something in the other hand it
is small at the side of the main hand (renderOneHandedMap), the same at any
pitch.

An item in the group held_map is now held that way: both arms
(renderMapHand; the left one with the left arm of a 64x64 skin, or the
right one mirrored on a 64x32 skin, as Minecraft converts old skins) and
the map on a quad in the wield scene, written in Minecraft's order and
frame like the other items: at (0, 0.04 - 0.5 tilt, -0.72) blocks, turned
by -85 tilt degrees, where tilt = 0.5 - 0.5 cos(pi clamp(1.1 - pitch / 45)).
The swing shifts it and turns it by 20 sin(sqrt(p) pi) degrees, and the
punch reload lowers it by 1.2 blocks instead of 0.6.

The map shows the HUD images named held_map, laid out as the HUD would
draw them and fitted to Minecraft's map with its border (142 of its 128
pixels), lit by the player's light like the arms. The HUD leaves them out
while the hands are drawn and draws them flat as before in third person or
with the hand hidden. The server keeps drawing the map as HUD images, so
the engine keeps no texture of its own.

The arm of an empty hand, and the arms holding a map, get the 0.1 radian
roll that Minecraft's renderHand gives them (1.8.9 too, from the idle sway
at age 0), which the arm patch had missed: the arm leans out slightly.

A wield mesh swapped at the bottom of the change animation was drawn for
one frame in the pose of the item before it (Camera::step runs after
Camera::update), and with a map the left arm missed that frame; the swap
now waits for the next update. The arms holding a map come as near as
0.05 block, Minecraft's near plane, so the wield camera's near plane is 3
units while a map is held (10 otherwise).

Checked in the native build at 640x480 against the outline of the map's
frame that Minecraft's own math gives: two hands at pitches from -30 to
80, during a swing and after a punch; one hand, with a shield in the
other; the flat fallback with the hand hidden; a sword swapped for the map
and back, frame by frame. The sword, a dirt block and a torch draw the same
as before; the empty arm differs by the roll.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

